  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and it passes `typecheck`. I could not run it in a browser or run `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. The camera orbit, shadows, fog strength and framing are untested.\n\n- **Instancing:** the forest is five draw nodes. Two of them cover the trees: 8,000 trunks (`instances.cylinder`) and 8,000 canopies (`instances.cone`). The other 2,000 are rocks (`instances.polyhedron`, icosahedron). The ground is a single 400×400 plane.\n- **Placement:** a seeded mulberry32 generator (seed 20240611) scatters everything uniformly over a disc of radius 48, so the forest is the same on every load.\n- **Colour variation:** each instance gets its own colour. Canopies range from yellow-green to blue-green, trunks are brown shades, and rocks are grey to tan. The materials are white so the per-instance colour comes through.\n- **Light and fog:** one directional sun casts shadows, using a 2048 shadow map, 2 cascades and an explicit `normalBias`. Only the canopies cast shadows. A hemisphere fill light supplies ambient light. Exponential fog uses density 0.006, with its colour matched to the background.\n- **Camera:** a tiny node named `orbit-pivot` sits at the forest centre. The follow camera is offset from it by (85, 62, 0) in `target-yaw` mode, and I rotate the pivot about Y at 0.1 rad/s. That is about one revolution per minute. I chose this because the library's `orbit` camera mode is static. The camera is set so the whole forest fits in frame at 640×360.\n- **Render loop:** I create the app with `autoStart: false`, `resize: false` and `pixelRatio: 1`, and wait for `app.ready()`. My own `requestAnimationFrame` loop then rotates the pivot and calls `app.step(dt)`. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first stepped frame.\n\nThe settings most likely to need adjusting after a first look at a frame are the fog density, sun intensity and the camera offset.\n\n",
